1
00:00:02,269 --> 00:00:03,931
NARRATOR: <i>Previously on</i> Gotham:

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,836
You haven't by chance
seen Officer Dougherty?

3
00:00:10,210 --> 00:00:11,234
[GRUNTS]

4
00:00:11,411 --> 00:00:14,245
Supposed to have dinner last
night and he didn't show. No surprise.

5
00:00:14,414 --> 00:00:17,384
- Um, but I haven't seen him today.
- Me neither.

6
00:00:17,718 --> 00:00:19,914
Barbara, we're
doing this for you.

7
00:00:20,153 --> 00:00:22,782
[GASPS THEN SCREAMS]

8
00:00:24,625 --> 00:00:28,118
- He's dead?
- Yeah. He's dead. It's gonna be okay.

9
00:00:28,562 --> 00:00:32,795
Bruce, your father was a true
stoic. He kept his best self hidden.

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,402
- Your father was a good man.
- But even good men have secrets.

11
00:00:36,803 --> 00:00:39,398
You have a hard head,
James, just like your father.

12
00:00:39,573 --> 00:00:43,374
Gotham is on a knife edge. But I
won't let it fall apart without a fight.

13
00:00:43,577 --> 00:00:45,337
ESSEN: Just got
wind of three attacks on

14
00:00:45,361 --> 00:00:47,412
Falcone businesses in
different parts of town.

15
00:00:47,581 --> 00:00:50,141
It's Maroni. We're
in a shooting war.

16
00:00:55,789 --> 00:00:57,348
[WOOD CREAKING]

17
00:01:15,342 --> 00:01:16,708
Whoa.

18
00:01:26,687 --> 00:01:28,280
Good morning, child.

19
00:01:29,923 --> 00:01:31,915
We ain't children.

20
00:01:32,125 --> 00:01:34,754
And it ain't morning.

21
00:01:35,128 --> 00:01:37,188
Oh, it will be soon.

22
00:01:40,033 --> 00:01:42,298
A brand-new day.

23
00:01:54,548 --> 00:01:57,712
NEWSMAN [ON TV]: <i>The gang
war rages on here in Gotham.</i>

24
00:01:57,884 --> 00:01:59,910
<i>No one knows what
set this turf war off...</i>

25
00:02:00,087 --> 00:02:03,990
<i>but it is widely believed to be the
result of the longtime power struggle...</i>

26
00:02:04,157 --> 00:02:07,924
<i>between Salvatore Maroni
and Carmine Falcone.</i>

27
00:02:08,095 --> 00:02:10,997
<i>Longtime enemies, these
men have often come to blows...</i>

28
00:02:12,199 --> 00:02:14,293
[NEWSMAN CONTINUES
SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

29
00:02:17,204 --> 00:02:19,070
ALFRED: Still looking
for secrets, are we, sir?

30
00:02:20,907 --> 00:02:23,103
Or just breaking stuff?

31
00:02:23,777 --> 00:02:25,370
Secrets.

32
00:02:25,579 --> 00:02:27,241
ALFRED: Why don't I...?

33
00:02:27,781 --> 00:02:29,773
Whole city's going
doolally around us.

34
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:33,076
I mean, there's a bloody
war on the streets...

35
00:02:34,488 --> 00:02:36,753
and you're here worrying
about your poor dad.

36
00:02:37,090 --> 00:02:38,524
Yes, I am.

37
00:02:38,725 --> 00:02:42,025
If your father had a secret life,
which I hasten to add he didn't...

38
00:02:42,195 --> 00:02:43,686
I'd know about it, wouldn't I?

39
00:02:44,231 --> 00:02:47,429
A sober, diligent man, he
was. Never out gallivanting.

40
00:02:47,634 --> 00:02:52,538
Home every night like clockwork,
working in this very room.

41
00:02:52,706 --> 00:02:54,436
Yes, I remember.

42
00:02:54,608 --> 00:02:57,134
I wasn't allowed to disturb
him while he was working.

43
00:02:57,310 --> 00:02:58,184
Nobody was.

44
00:02:58,208 --> 00:03:01,247
Classical music, he
would play, for hours.

45
00:03:01,448 --> 00:03:02,541
That's right.

46
00:03:02,716 --> 00:03:06,118
Mom and I would read books or
play board games until he was done.

47
00:03:06,319 --> 00:03:07,719
I remember.

48
00:03:07,921 --> 00:03:12,825
I tried to sneak in to see
him, but the door was locked.

49
00:03:14,394 --> 00:03:18,331
- Was the door always locked, Alfred?
- Yeah, I suppose so, when he was working.

50
00:03:18,498 --> 00:03:20,296
Why, though?

51
00:03:22,102 --> 00:03:25,072
- Why lock the door?
- That's what doors are for, isn't it?

52
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:29,439
It's in here. It's in this room.

53
00:03:29,910 --> 00:03:32,744
My father's secret.
It's in this room.

54
00:03:32,946 --> 00:03:36,246
I know it. I know it.

55
00:03:36,416 --> 00:03:39,113
Uh... Oh.

56
00:03:39,286 --> 00:03:40,948
[CHICKENS CLUCKING]

57
00:03:41,121 --> 00:03:42,612
[CHATTERING]

58
00:04:05,846 --> 00:04:10,807
Hello, young lady.
I like your style.

59
00:04:30,137 --> 00:04:32,902
COP 1: Put Falcone's guys in
one cell and Maroni's in another.

60
00:04:33,073 --> 00:04:35,099
[INDISTINCT SHOUTING
AND GRUNTING]

61
00:04:39,279 --> 00:04:40,372
COP 2: Shut up.

62
00:04:40,814 --> 00:04:42,146
Get in there.

63
00:04:42,649 --> 00:04:44,948
More where that came from.

64
00:04:59,599 --> 00:05:01,659
Officer Katz, right?

65
00:05:01,868 --> 00:05:04,565
- Yes, sir. Katz, Griffin.
- Word of advice, Katz.

66
00:05:05,372 --> 00:05:06,931
I'm a dead duck around here.

67
00:05:07,107 --> 00:05:10,009
Brass are gonna fire me as
soon as this gang war cools off.

68
00:05:10,177 --> 00:05:12,146
So don't salute me.

69
00:05:13,046 --> 00:05:14,514
Makes you look like a rube.

70
00:05:22,155 --> 00:05:24,215
- Hi. LESLIE: Detective.

71
00:05:24,424 --> 00:05:26,359
- Dr. Thompkins.
- Jim.

72
00:05:26,526 --> 00:05:28,427
Thanks for coming to see me.

73
00:05:28,862 --> 00:05:30,660
It's good to see
you looking so well.

74
00:05:30,831 --> 00:05:34,029
I am well. Much better.

75
00:05:34,234 --> 00:05:39,036
Yes, you are. This is your last
checkup. You are all healed, physically.

76
00:05:39,239 --> 00:05:41,538
But you need to get
some trauma counseling.

77
00:05:41,741 --> 00:05:46,270
Doc, I'm happy. I
am happy to be alive.

78
00:05:46,446 --> 00:05:51,714
Which is why, Jim, I didn't wanna
leave without saying thank you properly.

79
00:05:53,386 --> 00:05:55,321
Thank you for saving my life.

80
00:05:56,656 --> 00:05:58,454
I was glad to do it.

81
00:05:59,793 --> 00:06:01,159
LESLIE: Ms. Kean.

82
00:06:01,328 --> 00:06:04,958
Trauma counseling, like, yesterday. I
have several recommendations for you.

83
00:06:05,165 --> 00:06:07,964
- Really, doc, I'm fine.
- You're not fine.

84
00:06:08,168 --> 00:06:10,660
After what you've been
through, nobody would be fine.

85
00:06:11,204 --> 00:06:13,833
Fine. If you will
be my counselor.

86
00:06:14,741 --> 00:06:17,267
And we do it at my place.

87
00:06:18,311 --> 00:06:22,407
No. I have some trauma training,
but it's not my area of focus.

88
00:06:22,582 --> 00:06:26,849
I like you. Okay? I
can talk to you. Please.

89
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:34,154
Okay.

90
00:06:35,128 --> 00:06:36,756
- Deal.
- Thank you.

91
00:06:39,032 --> 00:06:40,364
Hmm?

92
00:06:41,134 --> 00:06:45,401
COP: All right, last warning
to all of you: don't be that guy.

93
00:06:45,605 --> 00:06:48,803
Hey, Jim, did you hear the
news? Don Falcone just got hit. Jim.

94
00:06:48,975 --> 00:06:51,069
- What?
- Falcone got hit. Twenty minutes ago.

95
00:06:51,278 --> 00:06:52,746
Maroni's boys blew up his car.

96
00:06:52,913 --> 00:06:54,973
- Wait, he's dead?
- No. He's at the hospital.

97
00:06:55,148 --> 00:06:56,616
But he'll be dead soon enough.

98
00:06:56,783 --> 00:06:59,275
Essen said City Hall and
the bigwigs okayed the hit.

99
00:06:59,452 --> 00:07:01,421
They switched their
support to Maroni.

100
00:07:01,621 --> 00:07:04,716
Maroni? That hothead?
They think he can run this city?

101
00:07:04,891 --> 00:07:06,120
[CELL PHONE RINGING]

102
00:07:06,293 --> 00:07:08,421
Someone had to go. This
war has been bad for business.

103
00:07:08,962 --> 00:07:10,430
Hello?

104
00:07:22,309 --> 00:07:23,641
[GRUNTING]

105
00:07:28,982 --> 00:07:30,610
Hello?

106
00:07:30,817 --> 00:07:32,308
Hello?

107
00:07:43,496 --> 00:07:45,692
[SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

108
00:07:51,338 --> 00:07:53,637
[DOOR OPENS THEN CLOSES]

109
00:07:58,678 --> 00:08:00,010
Good morning, good morning.

110
00:08:00,613 --> 00:08:05,608
Oh, Penguin. Thank God. Get me out of
here. Maroni's men will be here any minute.

111
00:08:05,785 --> 00:08:07,879
Yes, I imagine so. Heh.

112
00:08:08,088 --> 00:08:09,454
Mustn't stay long.

113
00:08:13,293 --> 00:08:16,286
I knew I'd regret it if I didn't
say goodbye to you in person.

114
00:08:24,304 --> 00:08:25,636
It was me, old friend.

115
00:08:27,207 --> 00:08:28,539
I did this to you.

116
00:08:32,412 --> 00:08:34,608
I started the war.

117
00:08:34,781 --> 00:08:37,148
From the day I met you,
I was planning your end.

118
00:08:37,884 --> 00:08:41,013
I'm sorry it has
to be in this...

119
00:08:42,122 --> 00:08:44,421
squalid little corner, but...

120
00:08:52,699 --> 00:08:54,065
[SPEAKS IN FRENCH]

121
00:08:55,435 --> 00:08:56,596
Why?

122
00:08:56,770 --> 00:09:00,172
Nothing personal,
I assure you, sir.

123
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:06,280
You have been a wise
mentor and a good friend.

124
00:09:09,849 --> 00:09:12,284
But business must come first.

125
00:09:14,788 --> 00:09:17,587
I'm gonna take
your place, old man.

126
00:09:17,757 --> 00:09:19,191
I'm gonna be the king of Gotham.

127
00:09:19,359 --> 00:09:20,418
[CHUCKLING]

128
00:09:20,593 --> 00:09:22,357
You?

129
00:09:22,929 --> 00:09:24,329
Never.

130
00:09:24,898 --> 00:09:27,060
You're going to burn in hell.

131
00:09:34,140 --> 00:09:35,870
I do worry about that.

132
00:09:39,379 --> 00:09:43,612
But you first, old friend.

133
00:09:44,384 --> 00:09:46,376
You first.

134
00:09:48,088 --> 00:09:49,989
Nobody move.

135
00:09:50,323 --> 00:09:51,689
[GRUNTS]

136
00:09:54,861 --> 00:09:57,421
- Walk away.
- Shut up.

137
00:09:57,630 --> 00:10:00,259
You're both under arrest
for attempted murder.

138
00:10:06,172 --> 00:10:11,201
Didn't you hear? Falcone is out.
By official decree. Everyone agreed.

139
00:10:11,444 --> 00:10:12,935
I heard.

140
00:10:13,146 --> 00:10:16,446
So release me, Jim.
Get on the winning side.

141
00:10:16,616 --> 00:10:20,576
Winning side, my ass. Maroni's
a fool. He can't replace me.

142
00:10:20,753 --> 00:10:23,279
A thousand rivals will
jump up to challenge him.

143
00:10:23,490 --> 00:10:27,427
There'll be civil war. If I
die now, Gotham falls apart.

144
00:10:27,594 --> 00:10:31,429
I agree. But can you turn things
around? Can you take back control?

145
00:10:31,598 --> 00:10:33,032
Of course.

146
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,491
Two days at my safe house.

147
00:10:35,668 --> 00:10:38,502
I need two days to
make some moves.

148
00:10:38,671 --> 00:10:42,005
Then I'll skin Maroni and
those disloyal scum like rabbits.

149
00:10:42,175 --> 00:10:43,575
OSWALD: Wake up, Napoleon.

150
00:10:43,743 --> 00:10:47,942
Maroni's louts are minutes away from
coming here and cutting your throat.

151
00:10:48,114 --> 00:10:52,017
And the thing is, Jim, they
aren't very fond of me either.

152
00:10:52,185 --> 00:10:55,622
So seriously, you
need to uncuff me. Jim?

153
00:11:04,197 --> 00:11:08,658
- Jim, where the hell did you run off to?
- Harv, I'm in the hospital with Falcone.

154
00:11:08,868 --> 00:11:11,929
- There's nobody here.
- No kidding. What are you doing there?

155
00:11:12,105 --> 00:11:15,007
You gotta get out. They'll be
coming back to finish him off.

156
00:11:15,175 --> 00:11:17,667
Yeah. Any moment now.

157
00:11:18,244 --> 00:11:20,873
- Unless I get him out of here.
- Jim, Jim, Jim.

158
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:24,380
<i>- I could use some help.</i>
- No way. Are you nuts?

159
00:11:24,684 --> 00:11:27,119
He's a bad man, but he's
the best bad man we got.

160
00:11:27,287 --> 00:11:31,224
<i>If Falcone loses control,
Maroni's too wild to run Gotham.</i>

161
00:11:31,391 --> 00:11:33,326
- There'll be anarchy.
<i>- Yeah, probably.</i>

162
00:11:33,493 --> 00:11:36,395
- But Falcone already lost control.
- And he'll get it back.

163
00:11:36,563 --> 00:11:40,056
Listen, you're all twisted over
what happened to Barbara.

164
00:11:40,266 --> 00:11:44,601
- You're not thinking straight.
- Yeah. Maybe.

165
00:11:44,804 --> 00:11:48,138
But I'm done in this town
anyhow. What do I have to lose?

166
00:11:48,308 --> 00:11:49,640
Your life.

167
00:11:49,809 --> 00:11:51,402
Huh? How about your life?

168
00:11:57,917 --> 00:12:02,912
What the hell are you doing here? I
thought the fix was in with you guys.

169
00:12:06,392 --> 00:12:07,724
[CHUCKLES]

170
00:12:16,769 --> 00:12:18,670
- Can you walk?
- If I have to.

171
00:12:18,838 --> 00:12:21,330
Let's get you out. I'll take
you to your safe house.

172
00:12:21,574 --> 00:12:24,738
My friend, you are
making a terrible mistake.

173
00:12:25,545 --> 00:12:29,539
I'm doing this for the city, not
you. You're the least worst option.

174
00:12:29,716 --> 00:12:33,346
If I didn't think you could take
back control, I'd let you die.

175
00:12:33,586 --> 00:12:36,215
- Just so we're clear.
- We're clear.

176
00:12:36,389 --> 00:12:40,053
You can't leave me here.
Maroni's men will kill me.

177
00:12:40,593 --> 00:12:43,825
- Possible.
- No. No, you arrested us.

178
00:12:44,063 --> 00:12:45,656
We are in your custody.

179
00:12:46,566 --> 00:12:51,869
- That's true.
- You owe me a favor, Jim. You owe me.

180
00:12:52,105 --> 00:12:55,098
- Damn it. LOEB: James Gordon?

181
00:12:56,709 --> 00:12:58,803
Detective Gordon?

182
00:13:10,923 --> 00:13:12,152
Don't kill them.

183
00:13:21,234 --> 00:13:24,329
Morning, fellas. What
can I help you with?

184
00:13:24,537 --> 00:13:28,235
Yes, Gordon. As usual,
wrong place, wrong time.

185
00:13:28,474 --> 00:13:29,908
Go. Now.

186
00:13:30,076 --> 00:13:33,240
Why? So these men
can kill Carmine Falcone?

187
00:13:33,413 --> 00:13:35,575
I have no knowledge of that.

188
00:13:35,748 --> 00:13:38,343
Go. That's an order.

189
00:13:38,918 --> 00:13:40,682
You're a disgrace, Loeb.

190
00:13:41,621 --> 00:13:46,059
I hope to see you behind
bars very soon or dead.

191
00:13:46,259 --> 00:13:49,195
- Whichever.
- Hope?

192
00:13:51,164 --> 00:13:54,828
It's for losers, Jim.

193
00:13:59,238 --> 00:14:02,868
He's all yours.

194
00:15:06,205 --> 00:15:07,639
[GRUNTS]

195
00:15:08,741 --> 00:15:12,769
Jim, Jim, don't shoot. It's me. We gotta
go. There are ambulances in the basement.

196
00:15:12,945 --> 00:15:14,641
Where's your man?

197
00:15:15,415 --> 00:15:16,713
Uh...

198
00:15:17,316 --> 00:15:18,409
About that.

199
00:15:18,584 --> 00:15:21,486
- We gotta take Penguin and Gilzean too.
- Penguin and Gilzean?

200
00:15:21,721 --> 00:15:24,589
I arrested them for attempted
murder. They're in my custody.

201
00:15:24,757 --> 00:15:27,591
Ugh! I don't even wanna argue
with you. Come on, let's go. Go.

202
00:15:28,461 --> 00:15:29,759
Tommy?

203
00:15:29,962 --> 00:15:31,191
Tommy?

204
00:15:31,364 --> 00:15:32,992
[TIRES SCREECHING]

205
00:15:34,434 --> 00:15:35,474
MAN: Boss! Boss, look out!

206
00:15:37,170 --> 00:15:39,503
Get down.

207
00:15:53,453 --> 00:15:55,149
Damn it.

208
00:16:03,563 --> 00:16:06,397
What exactly are we
looking for, Master Bruce?

209
00:16:06,866 --> 00:16:10,303
When I find it, I'll know.

210
00:16:28,921 --> 00:16:31,152
Nobody knows about
this place, right? Nobody?

211
00:16:31,357 --> 00:16:33,349
Nobody. Anybody who did is dead.

212
00:16:34,227 --> 00:16:35,786
We'll be safe here.

213
00:16:40,366 --> 00:16:41,766
Hello.

214
00:16:45,037 --> 00:16:46,198
What's up?

215
00:16:46,806 --> 00:16:48,468
[GARAGE DOOR OPENS]

216
00:16:57,517 --> 00:16:59,509
FISH: I know, I know.

217
00:17:00,353 --> 00:17:01,685
It's astonishing.

218
00:17:01,888 --> 00:17:04,722
Sometimes I astonish myself.

219
00:17:09,395 --> 00:17:13,856
I feel like, uh,
this is all a dream.

220
00:17:14,066 --> 00:17:15,432
I'm gonna wake up soon.

221
00:17:20,206 --> 00:17:23,665
Barbara, you were abducted.

222
00:17:24,577 --> 00:17:26,637
Your parents were
killed in front of you.

223
00:17:26,812 --> 00:17:29,043
Yeah. Yeah, that's real.

224
00:17:31,951 --> 00:17:34,921
I'm talking about
this, now. This...

225
00:17:35,655 --> 00:17:37,089
This is the dream.

226
00:17:38,057 --> 00:17:43,963
I'm gonna wake up,
and he's still alive.

227
00:17:45,798 --> 00:17:47,232
And he's coming for me.

228
00:17:52,972 --> 00:17:56,670
FISH: I thought for sure you
were dead. Let me look at you.

229
00:17:58,678 --> 00:18:00,169
Fish?

230
00:18:03,449 --> 00:18:05,441
Is it real...? Is it really you?

231
00:18:05,651 --> 00:18:09,383
It's me, Butch. I'm home.

232
00:18:11,290 --> 00:18:14,283
My poor baby. What
did they do to you?

233
00:18:16,295 --> 00:18:17,627
Nothing good.

234
00:18:19,432 --> 00:18:21,333
It's him, boss.

235
00:18:25,338 --> 00:18:27,204
Don Maroni.

236
00:18:28,474 --> 00:18:29,635
[CHUCKLES]

237
00:18:29,809 --> 00:18:33,337
Yes, alive and well.

238
00:18:34,313 --> 00:18:36,612
Well, that's a long story.

239
00:18:37,617 --> 00:18:39,449
We need to talk.

240
00:18:40,820 --> 00:18:43,619
I have something you want.

241
00:18:43,823 --> 00:18:45,655
[GRUNTING]

242
00:18:50,229 --> 00:18:51,993
They didn't find my knife.

243
00:18:54,066 --> 00:18:57,127
Ah. So we're saved, then.

244
00:18:57,336 --> 00:18:59,362
It's in my sock.

245
00:18:59,572 --> 00:19:01,905
If you get the chance,
take some of them with us.

246
00:19:02,074 --> 00:19:03,474
I'll try.

247
00:19:04,443 --> 00:19:06,674
- Hey, you.
- Selina, what are you doing here?

248
00:19:07,546 --> 00:19:10,812
- Chilling. How about you?
- Kind of jammed up.

249
00:19:11,484 --> 00:19:13,043
Yeah.

250
00:19:13,252 --> 00:19:14,652
That's too bad.

251
00:19:20,092 --> 00:19:26,862
Oh. That's mime for "Help us out here,"
and totally blow the coolest gig ever.

252
00:19:27,066 --> 00:19:32,562
Just because we kind of sort
of know each other? Right?

253
00:19:33,172 --> 00:19:34,367
[SIGHS]

254
00:19:36,275 --> 00:19:39,370
Hello, fellas. Hmm.

255
00:19:39,979 --> 00:19:41,777
Carmine.

256
00:19:44,417 --> 00:19:48,013
Penguin? Nothing to share?

257
00:19:48,220 --> 00:19:49,916
Well, I have a little something.

258
00:19:50,923 --> 00:19:54,382
I just made a sweet
deal with Maroni.

259
00:19:54,593 --> 00:19:57,722
He just told me I could
have all my territories back...

260
00:19:57,930 --> 00:19:59,694
and all I have
to do in return...

261
00:20:01,567 --> 00:20:04,059
is give him Carmine's head.

262
00:20:06,338 --> 00:20:09,308
Uh, Fish, I need to go lie down.

263
00:20:11,777 --> 00:20:15,145
What did you do to that
poor soul, you sadist?

264
00:20:15,381 --> 00:20:16,713
I assure you...

265
00:20:18,017 --> 00:20:23,957
You will die a slow, painful
death on account of Butch.

266
00:20:24,156 --> 00:20:25,454
You too, Carmine.

267
00:20:28,427 --> 00:20:29,451
You.

268
00:20:32,932 --> 00:20:34,025
We'll keep it simple.

269
00:20:35,601 --> 00:20:37,536
And you?

270
00:20:38,437 --> 00:20:41,464
We're cool. Any questions?

271
00:20:42,975 --> 00:20:44,136
How you gonna kill them?

272
00:20:48,581 --> 00:20:51,847
I love this girl.

273
00:20:52,051 --> 00:20:53,713
[FISH LAUGHS]

274
00:20:58,190 --> 00:21:00,159
BARBARA: <i>Jason
saw right through me.</i>

275
00:21:00,359 --> 00:21:04,592
He knew me instantly.
Like I was naked.

276
00:21:05,364 --> 00:21:06,923
Scary.

277
00:21:07,133 --> 00:21:08,761
But thrilling.

278
00:21:10,469 --> 00:21:16,636
And aren't all the
best guys a little scary?

279
00:21:18,010 --> 00:21:22,380
I was way more scared of Jim
Gordon, first date we went on.

280
00:21:23,449 --> 00:21:27,511
So butch and stern.

281
00:21:28,220 --> 00:21:31,952
That type, they often
have a bad temper.

282
00:21:32,391 --> 00:21:37,227
Hmm. But Jim was a sweetie.
Never laid a finger on me.

283
00:21:39,365 --> 00:21:42,529
It's kind of surprising.
Does he ever hit you?

284
00:21:43,302 --> 00:21:48,240
Me? No. Why? Why would
he hit anyone? Any woman?

285
00:21:48,908 --> 00:21:52,106
You know, out of passion.

286
00:21:55,614 --> 00:21:58,140
I'm sorry. I didn't mean to pry.

287
00:21:58,350 --> 00:22:00,319
Only, I heard you
and Jim were dating.

288
00:22:01,754 --> 00:22:03,347
No.

289
00:22:04,089 --> 00:22:09,756
Uh, yes. We've been on a few dates.
I'm sorry. I didn't think that you knew.

290
00:22:10,529 --> 00:22:13,682
If that makes you
uncomfortable in any way...

291
00:22:13,706 --> 00:22:15,126
No, don't be silly.

292
00:22:15,534 --> 00:22:17,400
I, uh... I'm happy for you.

293
00:22:18,838 --> 00:22:20,329
He's one of the good guys.

294
00:22:20,940 --> 00:22:23,068
Yeah, he is.

295
00:22:23,676 --> 00:22:24,700
Hey, you hungry?

296
00:22:35,221 --> 00:22:36,883
FISH: So there he is.

297
00:22:37,389 --> 00:22:42,589
Fish, you mysterious,
crazy, gorgeous killer, you.

298
00:22:42,795 --> 00:22:43,819
I love you.

299
00:22:43,996 --> 00:22:45,123
[FISH CHUCKLES]

300
00:22:49,201 --> 00:22:51,033
This...

301
00:22:51,237 --> 00:22:54,674
This is delicious. Finally.

302
00:22:54,874 --> 00:22:59,539
- You're hard to kill, old man.
- No. Your people are second-rate.

303
00:22:59,712 --> 00:23:00,771
[SCOFFS]

304
00:23:02,248 --> 00:23:06,083
Ms. Mooney, if I may, a brief
word. I know my life is forfeit.

305
00:23:06,285 --> 00:23:09,380
I speak not for
my sake but yours.

306
00:23:10,823 --> 00:23:14,123
Because after all, I still
love and respect you.

307
00:23:15,094 --> 00:23:19,623
Kill me if you must,
but keep Falcone alive.

308
00:23:19,832 --> 00:23:22,267
As soon as he's dead,
Maroni has no use for you.

309
00:23:22,468 --> 00:23:24,562
You are simply a
threat. He will kill you.

310
00:23:24,770 --> 00:23:25,863
Oh!

311
00:23:26,705 --> 00:23:27,764
You think?

312
00:23:27,940 --> 00:23:30,068
OSWALD: Why would he
need another boss in town?

313
00:23:30,276 --> 00:23:33,906
- Another rival?
- That's where you're wrong, smart guy.

314
00:23:34,113 --> 00:23:37,208
A, she's not a rival,
because she's not a boss.

315
00:23:37,416 --> 00:23:38,509
She's an underboss.

316
00:23:39,718 --> 00:23:43,348
An underboss takes
orders. I don't take orders.

317
00:23:43,589 --> 00:23:46,582
I know that. We're cool. Relax.

318
00:23:47,960 --> 00:23:50,828
- I'm relaxed.
- I don't think you are, babes.

319
00:23:54,066 --> 00:23:56,467
Please don't call me "babes."

320
00:23:56,669 --> 00:24:00,037
You see? Not relaxed.

321
00:24:00,272 --> 00:24:05,040
Babes? Really? It's
a term of endearment.

322
00:24:05,277 --> 00:24:07,769
It means I like you.

323
00:24:09,048 --> 00:24:11,745
Fine, I misspoke.

324
00:24:11,951 --> 00:24:17,356
You're not an underboss and you're
not a babe. You tell me what you are.

325
00:24:19,558 --> 00:24:22,392
What we are, Sal...

326
00:24:24,063 --> 00:24:25,156
are partners.

327
00:24:25,798 --> 00:24:29,257
Whatever you want. Partners.

328
00:24:29,969 --> 00:24:33,804
I'm Partner Number 1, and
you're Partner Number 2.

329
00:24:34,673 --> 00:24:40,670
That's the deal, right? I'm
number one, and you are...?

330
00:24:43,983 --> 00:24:47,545
- Number two. MARONI:
There you go. Simple math.

331
00:24:47,720 --> 00:24:50,280
One, two, babes. Oops.

332
00:24:50,856 --> 00:24:54,850
Sorry. That's the
last time, I swear.

333
00:25:00,933 --> 00:25:04,392
Guys, can you feel
the buzz in the air?

334
00:25:04,903 --> 00:25:06,838
That's victory.

335
00:25:07,039 --> 00:25:10,373
Redemption. Power.

336
00:25:10,576 --> 00:25:14,536
When this old man
dies, a new day begins.

337
00:25:14,747 --> 00:25:19,310
We will rule Gotham.
We're building a dynasty.

338
00:25:20,052 --> 00:25:25,855
We will whip this
town like a rented mule.

339
00:25:26,058 --> 00:25:27,219
Right, babes?

340
00:25:27,393 --> 00:25:28,918
[MEN CHUCKLING]

341
00:25:29,261 --> 00:25:30,285
[CHUCKLES]

342
00:25:30,462 --> 00:25:33,022
All right, relax.
I'm kidding you.

343
00:25:34,767 --> 00:25:36,861
Guys, no, seriously.

344
00:25:38,103 --> 00:25:39,571
Don't call her "babes."

345
00:25:40,205 --> 00:25:42,470
Or "toots" or what have you.

346
00:25:42,708 --> 00:25:44,199
It's a women's-lib thing.

347
00:25:48,981 --> 00:25:50,574
I am relaxed.

348
00:25:57,156 --> 00:25:58,681
[GUNFIRE]

349
00:25:58,924 --> 00:25:59,948
Come on!

350
00:26:00,159 --> 00:26:01,889
[ALL GRUNTING AND SHOUTING]

351
00:26:04,963 --> 00:26:06,591
GORDON: Let's go.

352
00:26:14,606 --> 00:26:16,598
We've been circling
around the subject...

353
00:26:16,809 --> 00:26:20,746
but haven't talked about
what actually happened to you.

354
00:26:20,946 --> 00:26:23,074
What was done to you.

355
00:26:23,282 --> 00:26:25,877
Lee, you're a nice lady.

356
00:26:28,153 --> 00:26:31,749
I could go there, but I'm not
sure you wanna go there with me.

357
00:26:32,591 --> 00:26:33,889
Don't worry about me.

358
00:26:42,935 --> 00:26:45,029
Has Jim told you he loves you?

359
00:26:45,504 --> 00:26:46,836
- Barbara...
- Fair is fair.

360
00:26:47,039 --> 00:26:49,099
And I'm telling you
all my little secrets.

361
00:26:50,676 --> 00:26:51,905
No, he hasn't.

362
00:26:52,678 --> 00:26:54,840
But he will, though, won't he?

363
00:26:55,614 --> 00:26:57,810
You can feel it coming.

364
00:26:58,317 --> 00:27:00,616
Come on, look at you.
You're gorgeous and smart.

365
00:27:00,819 --> 00:27:02,515
Of course he loves you.

366
00:27:03,122 --> 00:27:04,954
Do you love him?

367
00:27:05,157 --> 00:27:07,149
Barbara.

368
00:27:09,361 --> 00:27:10,727
Tell me what happened.

369
00:27:12,965 --> 00:27:14,729
[GUNFIRE AND SHOUTING NEARBY]

370
00:27:19,204 --> 00:27:23,141
If we can get to Seventh without being
spotted by Fish's people, we're home free.

371
00:27:23,342 --> 00:27:26,210
- Then what?
- We find a safe place, regroup.

372
00:27:26,445 --> 00:27:28,380
Maroni's death is a
lucky break for you.

373
00:27:28,580 --> 00:27:30,515
Spoken like a true mafioso.

374
00:27:31,049 --> 00:27:34,884
You're the least worst option. Like you
said, without you, the city falls apart.

375
00:27:35,087 --> 00:27:38,148
Yes, I said that.
And it might be true.

376
00:27:38,357 --> 00:27:39,848
But this has been a long day...

377
00:27:40,025 --> 00:27:43,154
and I've had a long time to
look at this world I've created.

378
00:27:44,096 --> 00:27:46,588
I'm done with this
business. I'm out.

379
00:27:46,832 --> 00:27:48,960
I've got a place down
south I can go to.

380
00:27:49,168 --> 00:27:51,831
- I'm sorry.
- You're sorry?

381
00:27:52,037 --> 00:27:56,566
I know, I'm letting you down.
I'm letting the city down. I know.

382
00:27:56,775 --> 00:27:58,615
[DOOR OPENS NEARBY]
[MEN SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

383
00:27:58,777 --> 00:28:00,075
MAN 1: Think I heard something.

384
00:28:00,445 --> 00:28:01,565
MAN 2: Yeah, flash this way.

385
00:28:02,347 --> 00:28:04,077
Down here, down here.

386
00:28:04,283 --> 00:28:07,082
Okay, plan B.

387
00:28:24,069 --> 00:28:27,506
So you got a place down
south, huh? Nice? On the water?

388
00:28:28,140 --> 00:28:31,133
- Yes.
- Any work for beat-up old cops?

389
00:28:31,777 --> 00:28:33,143
Sure.

390
00:28:33,378 --> 00:28:36,280
How's that sound, Jim?
Beaches, sunshine, fishing...

391
00:28:36,515 --> 00:28:37,608
Yeah. Tempting.

392
00:28:39,051 --> 00:28:40,075
Hello again.

393
00:28:42,788 --> 00:28:44,086
Cat got your tongue?

394
00:28:50,729 --> 00:28:52,459
Yes.

395
00:28:56,435 --> 00:28:59,132
Selina. Boys.

396
00:29:00,572 --> 00:29:02,905
- Bless you.
- Gotta hand it to you, Fish.

397
00:29:03,108 --> 00:29:04,974
You really know
how to stop a show.

398
00:29:05,177 --> 00:29:08,272
I couldn't help myself,
Harvey. The man vexed me.

399
00:29:08,480 --> 00:29:12,281
Crazy move. Possibly a
good move too. We'll see.

400
00:29:12,484 --> 00:29:14,476
The battlefield is in flux.

401
00:29:14,686 --> 00:29:17,679
You could be looking at
the new queen of Gotham.

402
00:29:17,923 --> 00:29:20,324
Congratulations, my dear.

403
00:29:20,492 --> 00:29:24,953
- I'm quitting, Fish. I'm out.
- Does this mean I don't have to kill you?

404
00:29:26,365 --> 00:29:30,996
You're going to retire someplace warm,
lay back, read your little funny papers?

405
00:29:31,203 --> 00:29:34,799
- That was the thought.
- Yeah, nice thought.

406
00:29:35,007 --> 00:29:36,532
[GUNFIRE]

407
00:29:53,525 --> 00:29:54,823
Please, no...

408
00:29:57,296 --> 00:30:00,596
Fish! Where are you?

409
00:30:06,171 --> 00:30:07,662
Go.

410
00:30:28,393 --> 00:30:33,331
Then he left me for a long time.

411
00:30:33,532 --> 00:30:35,194
Alone.

412
00:30:36,034 --> 00:30:37,935
And I slept.

413
00:30:39,037 --> 00:30:40,369
I don't know for how long.

414
00:30:41,206 --> 00:30:43,334
And then he woke me up.

415
00:30:43,542 --> 00:30:46,410
Road trip, he said.

416
00:30:47,512 --> 00:30:53,213
We were halfway
there when I realized...

417
00:30:56,221 --> 00:30:58,247
we were going to
my parents' house.

418
00:31:00,158 --> 00:31:01,717
Go on.

419
00:31:01,927 --> 00:31:05,056
Well, then he tied
them up to a chair.

420
00:31:07,065 --> 00:31:12,470
And, uh, he told
me to talk to them...

421
00:31:12,704 --> 00:31:14,570
about my childhood issues.

422
00:31:15,374 --> 00:31:16,569
So I did.

423
00:31:16,775 --> 00:31:21,372
I just told them whatever
came into my head.

424
00:31:22,214 --> 00:31:24,206
And what came into your head?

425
00:31:24,416 --> 00:31:30,083
You know, just stupid
kid stuff, I guess. Like...

426
00:31:31,590 --> 00:31:34,583
My mom used to call me her
little piggy when I was little.

427
00:31:35,327 --> 00:31:37,262
They threw away my posters.

428
00:31:38,063 --> 00:31:40,225
Never nurtured my self-esteem.

429
00:31:40,932 --> 00:31:44,994
Just slowly, steadily...

430
00:31:46,304 --> 00:31:48,967
grinding away at my soul.

431
00:31:50,909 --> 00:31:55,005
Trying to make me disappear.

432
00:31:56,948 --> 00:31:58,814
That's not kid stuff.

433
00:31:59,017 --> 00:32:00,918
To be fair to them...

434
00:32:03,155 --> 00:32:05,283
they simply never understood me.

435
00:32:07,292 --> 00:32:12,993
They have no clue who I am.

436
00:32:14,166 --> 00:32:19,605
Even when I was killing them,
they just gaped at me like fools.

437
00:32:19,805 --> 00:32:21,865
No understanding.

438
00:32:23,175 --> 00:32:28,614
Barbara, Jason Lennon killed
your parents. You didn't kill them.

439
00:32:31,283 --> 00:32:32,307
Yes, I did.

440
00:32:34,953 --> 00:32:39,152
I stabbed them several times.

441
00:32:40,392 --> 00:32:42,122
And then I slit their throats.

442
00:32:51,203 --> 00:32:53,900
- I'd like to leave now, please.
- You uncomfortable?

443
00:32:55,640 --> 00:32:57,666
How about now?

444
00:32:59,878 --> 00:33:01,005
[DOOR OPENS]

445
00:33:05,550 --> 00:33:06,984
[LOCK CLICKS]

446
00:33:09,254 --> 00:33:11,348
[BARBARA GRUNTING]
[BANGING ON DOOR]

447
00:33:11,523 --> 00:33:13,014
[BUSY SIGNAL ON PHONE]

448
00:33:19,231 --> 00:33:20,824
[ELEVATOR BELL DINGS]

449
00:33:24,369 --> 00:33:26,565
[DOG WHIMPERING]

450
00:33:31,843 --> 00:33:33,072
[GRUNTS]

451
00:33:39,317 --> 00:33:40,785
[YELLS]

452
00:33:42,020 --> 00:33:43,283
[BOTH YELLING AND GRUNTING]

453
00:33:58,803 --> 00:34:00,829
[BARBARA GROWLING WILDLY]

454
00:34:14,286 --> 00:34:15,754
Lee?

455
00:34:17,088 --> 00:34:19,148
She just... She just went crazy.

456
00:34:23,929 --> 00:34:26,865
I told him that
woman was trouble.

457
00:34:30,135 --> 00:34:31,501
[PANTING]

458
00:34:41,947 --> 00:34:43,381
[GRUNTING]

459
00:34:54,559 --> 00:34:55,583
[FISH YELLS]

460
00:34:58,129 --> 00:34:59,961
[BOTH YELL]

461
00:35:12,410 --> 00:35:15,005
Butch. About time.

462
00:35:15,213 --> 00:35:17,273
- Shoot her, Butch.
- Oh, really?

463
00:35:17,816 --> 00:35:20,911
Do as I say, Butch. Shoot her.

464
00:35:23,288 --> 00:35:25,189
What are you doing? Drop him.

465
00:35:26,458 --> 00:35:28,927
My friend, remember
your training.

466
00:35:29,361 --> 00:35:30,829
I order you to shoot her.

467
00:35:31,630 --> 00:35:35,328
Butch, don't listen to him.
He did something to your mind.

468
00:35:35,533 --> 00:35:38,697
I'm your girl.
Remember, I'm your girl.

469
00:35:38,937 --> 00:35:41,566
Kill her now!

470
00:35:42,407 --> 00:35:44,308
[BOTH YELL]

471
00:35:46,978 --> 00:35:48,810
[GROANING]

472
00:35:49,047 --> 00:35:50,743
FISH: Butch.

473
00:35:51,249 --> 00:35:53,218
Oh, my God, what did I do?

474
00:35:54,219 --> 00:35:58,589
I'm so sorry. I'm so sorry. I would
never hurt you. I love you, Fish.

475
00:35:58,823 --> 00:36:01,588
It's okay. It's not your
fault. I love you too.

476
00:36:01,826 --> 00:36:04,591
He messed with your
mind. It's not your fault.

477
00:36:05,063 --> 00:36:08,227
I'm gonna be fine. You know me.

478
00:36:08,400 --> 00:36:10,266
[OSWALD YELLS THEN BUTCH GRUNTS]

479
00:36:10,435 --> 00:36:11,869
[GROWLS]

480
00:36:14,973 --> 00:36:16,703
Goodbye, Fish.

481
00:36:17,676 --> 00:36:19,110
It's all good.

482
00:36:20,211 --> 00:36:21,804
[BOTH YELL]

483
00:36:22,747 --> 00:36:23,840
No!

484
00:36:24,015 --> 00:36:25,881
[FISH SCREAMS]

485
00:36:26,985 --> 00:36:28,180
[WATER SPLASHES]

486
00:36:37,595 --> 00:36:40,064
I'm the king of Gotham.

487
00:36:42,534 --> 00:36:44,162
[LAUGHING]

488
00:36:44,502 --> 00:36:48,496
I'm the king of Gotham!

489
00:36:48,707 --> 00:36:52,610
I'm the king of Gotham!

490
00:36:56,247 --> 00:36:58,148
Mr. Nygma.

491
00:36:58,383 --> 00:37:00,147
Ms. Kringle.

492
00:37:02,087 --> 00:37:05,251
I just noticed
something really weird.

493
00:37:05,690 --> 00:37:07,056
Oh?

494
00:37:11,396 --> 00:37:14,093
This note Officer
Dougherty left for me...

495
00:37:14,299 --> 00:37:17,030
The first letter of every
line spells out your name.

496
00:37:20,171 --> 00:37:21,469
You see?

497
00:37:21,673 --> 00:37:28,671
N-Y-G-M-A.

498
00:37:29,347 --> 00:37:30,645
Yes.

499
00:37:30,849 --> 00:37:33,250
How... How odd.

500
00:37:33,451 --> 00:37:36,285
What an amazing coincidence.

501
00:37:37,622 --> 00:37:40,592
- Coincidence?
- What else could it be?

502
00:37:42,927 --> 00:37:47,297
- So you know nothing about this?
- I? Me? No. No.

503
00:37:48,433 --> 00:37:49,992
No.

504
00:37:51,636 --> 00:37:53,104
Huh.

505
00:38:03,982 --> 00:38:05,473
[DOOR CLOSES]

506
00:38:09,387 --> 00:38:10,855
[LAUGHING]

507
00:38:14,793 --> 00:38:16,569
DOUGHERTY: <i>You're
the guy that likes riddles.</i>

508
00:38:16,593 --> 00:38:17,713
- Why laugh? It's not funny.

509
00:38:17,862 --> 00:38:19,387
DOUGHERTY: <i>What a creep.</i>

510
00:38:19,697 --> 00:38:21,962
She'll keep digging.

511
00:38:22,300 --> 00:38:25,031
Why did you have
to leave a clue? Why?

512
00:38:25,403 --> 00:38:27,099
Relax.

513
00:38:27,305 --> 00:38:32,107
What can she find? What can she prove?
Nothing. There's no body. Stop worrying.

514
00:38:33,611 --> 00:38:36,342
She looked so sad and angry.
I hate it when she's that way.

515
00:38:36,514 --> 00:38:37,994
KRINGLE: <i>Menacing and weird.</i>
- Please.

516
00:38:38,183 --> 00:38:41,153
Like it matters. Like you
stand a chance with her.

517
00:38:41,352 --> 00:38:44,516
She knows how you feel about
her, and she treats you like dirt.

518
00:38:44,689 --> 00:38:45,713
[SIGHS]

519
00:38:45,890 --> 00:38:47,688
I don't care. I
still love her. Ugh!

520
00:38:47,859 --> 00:38:50,124
Listen to yourself. Be a man!

521
00:38:50,361 --> 00:38:51,841
KRINGLE: <i>You're an
odd man, Mr. Nygma.</i>

522
00:38:51,996 --> 00:38:54,329
You'll do better if she's
a little scared of you.

523
00:38:54,499 --> 00:38:56,491
- Stop talking like that.
DOUGHERTY: <i>No riddles.</i>

524
00:38:56,701 --> 00:38:59,101
KRINGLE: <i>Formal complaints about you.</i>
- Just keeping it real.

525
00:38:59,237 --> 00:39:02,139
What's black and white
and red all over? Stop it!

526
00:39:02,407 --> 00:39:04,535
DOUGHERTY: <i>I wouldn't
quit your day job if I were you.</i>

527
00:39:04,709 --> 00:39:06,302
[LAUGHING]

528
00:39:11,683 --> 00:39:14,016
KRINGLE: <i>He is so weird.</i>

529
00:39:27,732 --> 00:39:31,669
Beautiful, huh? I'm
gonna miss this town.

530
00:39:31,870 --> 00:39:33,805
You won't change your mind?

531
00:39:35,039 --> 00:39:36,063
Town needs you.

532
00:39:36,274 --> 00:39:42,111
No. Gotham needs a lawman
now, not a criminal like me.

533
00:39:43,748 --> 00:39:44,807
A strong lawman.

534
00:39:48,486 --> 00:39:49,749
You looking at me?

535
00:39:49,954 --> 00:39:53,447
Someone needs to grab this
city by the neck and shake it hard.

536
00:39:53,691 --> 00:39:57,253
That's you, Jim. I
know it in my bones.

537
00:39:57,896 --> 00:40:01,128
There's a lot of powerful men
who would disagree with you on that.

538
00:40:06,804 --> 00:40:08,295
FALCONE: Nice knife, huh?

539
00:40:16,347 --> 00:40:20,079
That was your father's. He
gave it to me a long time ago.

540
00:40:20,285 --> 00:40:23,813
A spur-of-the-moment
birthday gift. I said no.

541
00:40:24,722 --> 00:40:28,625
Such a beautiful knife, and I
have men with guns to protect me.

542
00:40:28,826 --> 00:40:30,795
He said, "Take it.

543
00:40:30,995 --> 00:40:33,521
A knife is a good friend
when you have no other."

544
00:40:34,799 --> 00:40:36,734
You were that close?

545
00:40:38,136 --> 00:40:39,536
At one time.

546
00:40:44,943 --> 00:40:47,003
Point of the story, Jim...

547
00:40:47,245 --> 00:40:50,306
your father was the most
honest man I ever met.

548
00:40:51,249 --> 00:40:53,377
But he carried a knife.

549
00:41:08,766 --> 00:41:11,759
ALFRED: Enough searching,
Master B. There's nothing here.

550
00:41:15,073 --> 00:41:18,510
Now, your father was a good
man. A flawed man like anyone else.

551
00:41:18,710 --> 00:41:22,374
- Doesn't mean he had a secret life.
- My intuition is nearly always correct.

552
00:41:22,580 --> 00:41:24,173
Yeah, well...

553
00:41:24,382 --> 00:41:26,908
It's a common delusion, I find.

554
00:41:28,720 --> 00:41:31,690
Arkham's full of men whose
intuitions were always correct.

555
00:41:33,725 --> 00:41:35,091
There are none so blind.

556
00:41:41,366 --> 00:41:42,561
None so blind.

557
00:41:44,836 --> 00:41:47,931
- Marcus Aurelius. Marcus Aurelius.
- What about him?

558
00:41:50,341 --> 00:41:51,508
He was a Roman emperor.

559
00:41:51,532 --> 00:41:53,675
Yes, I'm fully aware
of that, Master Bruce.

560
00:41:53,878 --> 00:41:54,902
And a stoic.

561
00:41:55,113 --> 00:41:57,878
I think I remember he
was a stoic, like my father.

562
00:41:59,350 --> 00:42:01,876
Lucius Fox called
my father a stoic.

563
00:42:02,086 --> 00:42:05,250
Lots of people are called stoic.

564
00:42:05,456 --> 00:42:08,688
It means a hard nut who doesn't
say much. Doesn't mean it's a clue.

565
00:42:09,193 --> 00:42:12,129
It's a philosophy,
Alfred. It's a way of life.

566
00:42:12,930 --> 00:42:15,832
Maybe... Maybe that
as well, I suppose, but...

567
00:42:19,237 --> 00:42:21,001
- Ah!
- Uh-oh.

568
00:42:28,913 --> 00:42:31,280
- What is it, do you think?
- I don't know and I don't care.

569
00:42:31,482 --> 00:42:33,678
- Don't you dare press that button.
- Why not?

570
00:42:34,152 --> 00:42:35,780
It could be a bomb.

571
00:42:35,953 --> 00:42:38,252
Alfred, that seems improbable.

572
00:42:39,690 --> 00:42:42,353
[PROKOFIEV'S "DANCE OF THE
KNIGHTS" PLAYING OVER SPEAKERS]

573
00:42:45,496 --> 00:42:46,862
It's a stereo.

574
00:42:48,166 --> 00:42:49,464
[BRICKS GRINDING]

575
00:44:04,342 --> 00:44:06,334
[English - US -SDH]
